#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Written one-line takeaways for John.

Each states what the verse turns on. Narrative verses that carry no
standalone point are left out deliberately; the drawer hides the card
rather than showing filler.
"""

TAKEAWAYS = {
    # ---- Chapter 1 ----
    '1:1': "John starts earlier than Genesis does. Before anything began, the Word already was, and was God.",
    '1:2': "The line is repeated because it is the hinge: he was with God at the beginning, not made along the way.",
    '1:3': "Everything that exists was made through him. There is no neutral ground he did not make.",
    '1:4': "Life first, then light. What he gives is not information about living but the living itself.",
    '1:5': "The darkness did not comprehend it and did not put it out. The shining is in the present tense.",
    '1:6': "\"A man sent from God, whose name was John.\" After the eternal Word, a man with an errand.",
    '1:7': "The witness exists so others might believe. His work is pointing, not being looked at.",
    '1:8': "\"He was not that Light.\" John's greatness lies in knowing exactly what he is not.",
    '1:9': "The true Light lights every man. No one arrives in the world already outside its reach.",
    '1:10': "He made the world and the world knew him not. This ignorance is not a shortage of evidence.",
    '1:11': "\"His own received him not.\" The rejection did not come from strangers; it happened at home.",
    '1:12': "Received, believed, given. Sonship is handed over as a gift, never earned into.",
    '1:13': "Not of blood, nor of the will of the flesh, nor of man. Nobody talks his way into this birth.",
    '1:14': "The Word was made flesh and pitched his tent among us. God did not stay at a safe distance.",
    '1:15': "\"He was before me.\" John, the elder in years, hands the precedence over on grounds of eternity.",
    '1:16': "\"Grace for grace\" — grace replacing grace, a supply that does not run down to the bottom.",
    '1:17': "Law by Moses, grace and truth by Christ. Both are God's giving, and they do different work.",
    '1:18': "No man has seen God; the Son has declared him. Christ is not a window onto God but the telling of him.",
    '1:20': "\"He confessed, and denied not.\" The first thing he confesses is what he is not.",
    '1:21': "Three titles offered, three refused. He will not take a larger name than the one he was given.",
    '1:22': "They need something to bring back. The question is asked to file a report, not to learn.",
    '1:23': "\"I am the voice.\" Not the message and not the man — a sound that clears the way for someone else.",
    '1:25': "The challenge is about licence. If he is nobody in particular, by what right does he baptize?",
    '1:26': "\"There standeth one among you, whom ye know not.\" Christ is already present and already unrecognized.",
    '1:27': "Loosing the sandal was slave's work. John says he is not fit for even that much.",
    '1:29': "\"Behold the Lamb of God.\" Sacrifice is named before the ministry starts, and the sin is the world's.",
    '1:31': "\"I knew him not.\" The forerunner did not recognize his own kinsman until he was shown.",
    '1:32': "The Spirit descended and abode. The remaining is the point, not the descent.",
    '1:33': "John baptizes with water; the one he points at baptizes with the Holy Ghost. He knows which is lesser.",
    '1:34': "\"I saw, and bare record.\" Testimony reports what was seen; it is not an opinion offered.",
    '1:36': "He says it a second time, and his own disciples walk off after Jesus. He built that outcome.",
    '1:38': "\"What seek ye?\" The first words Jesus speaks here are a question about what a man actually wants.",
    '1:39': "\"Come and see.\" The invitation is an afternoon in his company, not an argument.",
    '1:41': "Andrew's first act after finding the Messiah is to go and fetch his brother.",
    '1:42': "Jesus renames him before he has done anything to earn it. The stone is a promise, not a report.",
    '1:43': "\"Follow me.\" Philip is found rather than recruited; the finding is on Jesus' side.",
    '1:45': "Philip's whole summary is \"the son of Joseph\" — half right, and enough to get Nathanael moving.",
    '1:46': "\"Can there any good thing come out of Nazareth?\" The sneer is met with an invitation, not a defence.",
    '1:47': "\"An Israelite indeed, in whom is no guile\" — praise handed to a man who has just been rude.",
    '1:48': "\"I saw thee.\" What undoes Nathanael is not an argument but discovering he was known first.",
    '1:49': "In one verse he goes from mocking Nazareth to confessing the Son of God.",
    '1:50': "Faith resting on one small wonder is accepted as it stands, and promised far more than it asked.",
    '1:51': "Jacob's ladder, with the Son of man where the ladder stood. Heaven's traffic runs through him now.",

    # ---- Chapter 2 ----
    '2:1': "A village wedding is where the glory first shows. The ordinary is not beneath him.",
    '2:3': "\"They have no wine.\" She names the lack and asks for nothing. The telling is the asking.",
    '2:4': "\"Mine hour is not yet come.\" The whole Gospel runs on a clock that stops at the cross.",
    '2:5': "\"Whatsoever he saith unto you, do it.\" Rebuffed, she hands the servants over to him anyway.",
    '2:6': "Six stone jars kept for ritual washing. The water of the old purifying is what he turns.",
    '2:7': "\"Fill the waterpots with water.\" The servants' share is plain labour; the miracle is not theirs.",
    '2:8': "They carry it to the steward before anyone has tasted it. Obedience runs out ahead of proof.",
    '2:9': "The steward praises wine whose source he cannot name, and the servants who drew it say nothing.",
    '2:10': "\"Thou hast kept the good wine until now.\" God's order runs opposite the world's: best comes last.",
    '2:11': "John calls it a sign rather than a wonder. What matters is what it showed, and it showed glory.",
    '2:14': "The trade is inside the temple court and it is all sacrificial. Religion running as a business.",
    '2:15': "A whip of cords and the tables over. The meekness of Christ is not an inability to be angry.",
    '2:16': "\"My Father's house.\" He clears it as a son clearing his father's home, not as a reformer with a cause.",
    '2:17': "The disciples reach for a psalm to make sense of what they have just watched him do.",
    '2:18': "They demand a sign to license the act, as if his authority were something to produce on request.",
    '2:19': "\"Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up.\" The only sign offered is his own death.",
    '2:20': "Forty-six years of building set against three days. They measure him by the labour they have invested.",
    '2:21': "The temple he means is his body. The meeting place of God and man has changed address.",
    '2:22': "They understood it afterwards. Faith often runs behind the event rather than ahead of it.",
    '2:23': "Many believed because of the miracles. John records it without once calling it enough.",
    '2:24': "\"Jesus did not commit himself unto them.\" He entrusts himself to no crowd, however warm.",
    '2:25': "He knew what was in man. Nothing about us has ever been news to him, and he came anyway.",

    # ---- Chapter 3 ----
    '3:1': "A Pharisee and a ruler: the man with everything religion can award is the one who comes asking.",
    '3:2': "He comes by night and opens with what \"we know.\" What he knows will not carry him far.",
    '3:3': "Not improved, not instructed — born. Without that, the kingdom is not even visible.",
    '3:4': "Nicodemus takes it literally and is not wrong to call it impossible. Birth is done to you.",
    '3:5': "Water and the Spirit. The new birth comes through a means, not through an inward effort.",
    '3:6': "Flesh produces flesh. Nothing in human nature can make this leap out of its own stock.",
    '3:7': "\"Ye must.\" It is stated as a necessity, not recommended as an improvement.",
    '3:8': "The wind blows where it wills and you hear it without steering it. The Spirit is not managed.",
    '3:9': "\"How can these things be?\" A man asking for the method of something that is done to him.",
    '3:10': "\"Art thou a master of Israel, and knowest not these things?\" Israel's own scriptures had said it.",
    '3:11': "Testimony given and testimony refused — the shape of the whole Gospel, stated this early.",
    '3:12': "If the earthly is not believed, the heavenly will not be either. Unbelief is not cured by more data.",
    '3:13': "No one has climbed up. Revelation comes downward, from the one who came down to bring it.",
    '3:14': "The cure in the wilderness was to look at the thing that was killing them, lifted up. So the cross.",
    '3:15': "\"Whosoever believeth.\" The looking is the whole condition, and looking is not a work.",
    '3:16': "God so loved the world — not the lovely, not the likely. The giving came before any response.",
    '3:17': "He was not sent to condemn. Condemnation was already in hand; rescue is what had to be sent.",
    '3:18': "\"Condemned already.\" Unbelief is not a verdict still pending; it is the standing situation.",
    '3:19': "The condemnation is not that light was withheld, but that it came and men preferred the dark.",
    '3:20': "Evil hates the light for what it would show. The avoidance is deliberate, not accidental.",
    '3:21': "He that doeth truth comes to the light, content that his deeds be shown as God's work and not his own.",
    '3:25': "A dispute about purifying turns, within a verse, into a dispute about who is drawing the crowds.",
    '3:26': "\"All men come to him.\" John's disciples report the success of Christ as though it were a loss.",
    '3:27': "\"A man can receive nothing, except it be given him from heaven.\" That covers his own ministry too.",
    '3:29': "The bridegroom's friend is glad at another man's wedding. Joy that does not need to be central.",
    '3:30': "\"He must increase, but I must decrease.\" A ministry measured by how far it gets out of the way.",
    '3:31': "Origin settles authority. What is of the earth can only ever speak of the earth.",
    '3:32': "\"No man receiveth his testimony\" — said of the one witness who reports nothing but what he saw.",
    '3:33': "To receive the testimony is to set a seal that God is true. Faith is a verdict on God's honesty.",
    '3:34': "The Spirit is given him without measure. Nothing is rationed where Christ is concerned.",
    '3:35': "The Father loves the Son and has put all things in his hand. Where you stand with Christ is where you stand.",
    '3:36': "Everlasting life in the present tense — and the wrath that abides was already there, not newly earned.",
}

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 4 ----
    '4:4': "\"He must needs go through Samaria.\" The necessity is not geography; it is one woman at a well.",
    '4:6': "Wearied with his journey, he sits down at noon. The incarnation includes being tired.",
    '4:7': "\"Give me to drink.\" He opens by needing something from her rather than by offering.",
    '4:9': "She names every barrier at once — race, sex, custom — and he has already stepped over all three.",
    '4:10': "\"If thou knewest the gift of God.\" Her trouble is not refusal but not knowing who is asking.",
    '4:11': "\"The well is deep.\" She is still measuring him by rope and bucket.",
    '4:12': "\"Art thou greater than our father Jacob?\" Asked as a challenge; the answer happens to be yes.",
    '4:13': "Water that leaves you thirsty again describes a good deal more than a well.",
    '4:14': "The gift becomes a spring inside the one who drinks. It is given, never fetched.",
    '4:15': "She asks for the water so as to stop walking out here. Half-understanding is still asking.",
    '4:16': "\"Go, call thy husband.\" The conversation cannot go further until the avoided thing is named.",
    '4:17': "\"Thou hast well said.\" He takes her evasion and agrees with it, which is worse than contradiction.",
    '4:18': "Five husbands and a sixth who is not. He states the whole record without a syllable of scolding.",
    '4:19': "\"I perceive that thou art a prophet.\" Being known exactly is what convinces her.",
    '4:20': "The moment it gets personal she raises a theological dispute. The manoeuvre is very old.",
    '4:21': "An hour is coming when the question of which mountain will simply stop mattering.",
    '4:22': "\"Salvation is of the Jews.\" He will not flatten the difference to make the talk more comfortable.",
    '4:23': "\"The Father seeketh such to worship him.\" The seeking begins on God's side of the well.",
    '4:24': "God is Spirit, so worship has no address. That does not make it vague; it makes it true or false.",
    '4:25': "She retreats to a Messiah who is coming later. He is sitting in front of her.",
    '4:26': "\"I that speak unto thee am he.\" The plainest claim in the Gospel, made to one Samaritan woman.",
    '4:27': "They marvel that he talks with a woman, and not one of them dares ask why. Propriety keeps quiet.",
    '4:28': "She leaves the waterpot behind. What she walked out here for is forgotten on the spot.",
    '4:29': "\"A man, which told me all things that ever I did.\" Her shame has turned into her testimony.",
    '4:32': "\"I have meat to eat that ye know not of.\" He is fed by something the disciples cannot see.",
    '4:33': "They assume somebody brought lunch. They hear him at the flattest level available.",
    '4:34': "\"My meat is to do the will of him that sent me.\" Obedience as nourishment, not as a load carried.",
    '4:35': "Four months is the sensible estimate. He says look now — the Samaritans are already on the road.",
    '4:36': "Sower and reaper rejoice together. The work is not split into the fruitful part and the rest.",
    '4:37': "\"One soweth, and another reapeth.\" Most labour is handed on unfinished, and that is normal.",
    '4:38': "\"Ye are entered into their labours.\" No one's ministry starts from bare ground.",
    '4:39': "A town believes on the word of a woman with a reputation. God is not fastidious about instruments.",
    '4:41': "More believed at his own word. Testimony gets people there; it was never meant to hold them.",
    '4:42': "\"We have heard him ourselves.\" Borrowed faith becomes their own, and they call him Saviour of the world.",
    '4:44': "He quotes the proverb about a prophet's own country and then goes back to it anyway.",
    '4:45': "The Galilaeans welcome him for what they saw in Jerusalem. A reception built on spectacle.",
    '4:47': "A court official walks a day's road to beg. Rank stops counting when a child is dying.",
    '4:48': "\"Except ye see signs and wonders, ye will not believe.\" Said to a desperate man, and not softened.",
    '4:49': "\"Sir, come down ere my child die.\" He still assumes the healing needs Jesus to be in the room.",
    '4:50': "He is given a sentence instead of a journey, and believes the word with no evidence in hand.",
    '4:52': "He asks them for the hour. Faith is not afraid to check the timing.",
    '4:53': "The hour matched, and his whole house believed with him. One man's faith carried a household.",

    # ---- Chapter 5 ----
    '5:3': "A crowd of blind, halt and withered, all waiting for the water to move. Religion as a queue.",
    '5:5': "Thirty-eight years. Long enough that nothing was going to change of its own accord.",
    '5:6': "\"Wilt thou be made whole?\" Not an idle question; a long sickness can become a way of life.",
    '5:7': "\"I have no man.\" His answer is not yes but an account of why it has never worked.",
    '5:8': "\"Rise, take up thy bed, and walk.\" No water, no queue, no merit — a command carrying its own power.",
    '5:9': "He is healed on the sabbath, and John names the day because the trouble starts right there.",
    '5:10': "A man walks after thirty-eight years and the first response is a complaint about his bedding.",
    '5:11': "He answers with the authority behind the act: the one who healed him told him to carry it.",
    '5:12': "They do not ask who healed him. They ask who told him to carry a bed.",
    '5:13': "He was made whole without knowing who did it. The gift arrived before the acquaintance.",
    '5:14': "\"Sin no more, lest a worse thing come unto thee.\" The body was the smaller problem all along.",
    '5:15': "Once he knows the name he takes it straight to the authorities. Gratitude is not automatic.",
    '5:16': "The persecution begins over sabbath practice, which is how these things usually begin.",
    '5:17': "\"My Father worketh hitherto, and I work.\" God's holding of the world in being takes no day off.",
    '5:18': "His hearers understood him better than his gentler defenders: he was claiming equality with God.",
    '5:19': "\"The Son can do nothing of himself.\" The claim to deity comes wrapped in total dependence.",
    '5:20': "The Father shows the Son everything he does. Not delegation — love that withholds nothing.",
    '5:21': "Raising the dead is God's own prerogative, and it is handed to the Son without qualification.",
    '5:22': "All judgment is committed to the Son. The one who will judge is the one who was judged.",
    '5:23': "Honour withheld from the Son is not honour paid to the Father by some other route.",
    '5:24': "Heareth, believeth, hath — and is passed from death unto life already. The tense is the comfort.",
    '5:25': "\"The hour is coming, and now is.\" The dead hearing his voice has already begun.",
    '5:26': "The Son has life in himself, given him. Nothing he holds is seized; all of it is received.",
    '5:27': "Judgment is his because he is the Son of man. We will be judged by one who was one of us.",
    '5:28': "The graves will hear the same voice that the living are busy refusing.",
    '5:29': "Two resurrections, one voice. That all rise is not in question; what they rise to is.",
    '5:30': "\"I seek not mine own will.\" His judgment is just because nothing of his own is at stake in it.",
    '5:31': "He concedes their rule of evidence rather than demanding belief on his own say-so.",
    '5:34': "\"That ye might be saved.\" He marshals witnesses not to win the argument but to rescue the opponents.",
    '5:35': "\"Willing for a season to rejoice in his light.\" John was a novelty they enjoyed and then dropped.",
    '5:36': "The works are the witness. What he does testifies to who sent him doing it.",
    '5:37': "\"Ye have neither heard his voice at any time.\" Their religion has never actually met God.",
    '5:38': "The test of having God's word in you is whether you receive the one he sent.",
    '5:39': "They searched the scriptures for life and walked past the one the scriptures were about.",
    '5:40': "\"Ye will not come to me, that ye might have life.\" The obstacle is not ignorance but unwillingness.",
    '5:41': "\"I receive not honour from men.\" He is not working for the approval they are withholding.",
    '5:42': "He does not charge them with bad arguments but with not having the love of God in them.",
    '5:43': "One who comes in his own name will be received. Self-assertion is easier to welcome than sending.",
    '5:44': "Honour traded among themselves makes belief impossible. Two audiences cannot both be served.",
    '5:45': "Moses, whom they trust, is the accuser. The law they lean on is the thing that indicts them.",
    '5:46': "\"He wrote of me.\" To believe Moses rightly is to end up at Christ.",
    '5:47': "If the writings are not believed, the words will not be either. The problem was never the medium.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 6 ----
    '6:2': "The crowd follows for the healings. John keeps noting what draws people, and it is not the word.",
    '6:4': "\"The passover was nigh.\" John sets the talk of flesh and blood against the feast of the lamb.",
    '6:5': "He asks Philip where bread can be bought, in a place where there is nowhere to buy it.",
    '6:6': "\"He himself knew what he would do.\" The test was for Philip's sake, not for information.",
    '6:7': "Philip does the arithmetic and gets the right answer: not enough. Accounting is not unbelief, but it is not faith.",
    '6:9': "\"But what are they among so many?\" Andrew brings the little he found and doubts it in one breath.",
    '6:10': "\"Make the men sit down.\" Five thousand are seated before there is anything at all to eat.",
    '6:11': "He gives thanks over five loaves in front of five thousand, and then begins handing them out.",
    '6:12': "\"That nothing be lost.\" The one who just made plenty out of nothing refuses to waste the scraps.",
    '6:13': "Twelve baskets left from five loaves. What remains is larger than what he started with.",
    '6:14': "They identify him correctly as the Prophet and draw entirely the wrong conclusion from it.",
    '6:15': "They would crown him by force, so he leaves. A throne on man's terms is a temptation refused.",
    '6:17': "\"It was now dark, and Jesus was not come to them.\" John notes the absence before the storm.",
    '6:19': "They see him walking on the sea and are afraid. His nearness frightened before it comforted.",
    '6:20': "\"It is I; be not afraid.\" The words are the divine name, spoken over open water.",
    '6:21': "They receive him aboard, and at once the ship is where it had been struggling to go.",
    '6:24': "They take boats and hunt him down. Effort spent seeking proves nothing about why one seeks.",
    '6:25': "\"When camest thou hither?\" They ask about the mechanics; he answers about their motives.",
    '6:26': "\"Because ye did eat of the loaves, and were filled.\" He names their appetite instead of praising their zeal.",
    '6:27': "Labour not for the meat that perishes. The bread that lasts is given by the Son, not earned at work.",
    '6:28': "\"What shall we do?\" The instinctive question is always about works to be performed.",
    '6:29': "\"This is the work of God, that ye believe.\" The only work named is the one God does in you.",
    '6:30': "They ask for a sign the day after eating one. Signs never satisfy the man who demands them.",
    '6:31': "They hold up the manna as the standard Moses met. They are grading him against the wrong baker.",
    '6:32': "\"Moses gave you not that bread.\" Even the manna was never Moses' to hand out.",
    '6:33': "The bread of God is a person who came down, not a substance distributed from above.",
    '6:34': "\"Evermore give us this bread.\" They ask correctly and have no notion what they are asking for.",
    '6:35': "\"I am the bread of life.\" Coming to him and believing on him are set down as the same act.",
    '6:36': "\"Ye also have seen me, and believe not.\" Seeing has never been sufficient to produce faith.",
    '6:37': "\"Him that cometh to me I will in no wise cast out.\" Assurance rests on his refusal, not on your grip.",
    '6:38': "He came not to do his own will. The Son's whole errand is somebody else's will carried out.",
    '6:39': "\"That of all which he hath given me I should lose nothing.\" Keeping them is part of the assignment.",
    '6:40': "Seeth, believeth, hath, raised. The whole chain is the Father's will, and none of it is our doing.",
    '6:41': "They murmur, exactly as their fathers did in the wilderness, at bread come down from heaven.",
    '6:42': "\"Whose father and mother we know.\" Familiarity is produced as a decisive argument against him.",
    '6:44': "\"No man can come to me, except the Father draw him.\" Faith starts outside the believer.",
    '6:45': "\"They shall be all taught of God.\" Anyone actually taught by the Father ends up at Christ.",
    '6:46': "Only the one who is from God has seen the Father. Our knowledge of God runs through him or not at all.",
    '6:47': "\"He that believeth on me hath everlasting life.\" Present possession, with nothing added as a condition.",
    '6:49': "\"Your fathers did eat manna... and are dead.\" The best bread of the old covenant still ended in graves.",
    '6:50': "Bread a man may eat and not die. The comparison with the manna is exact and unflattering.",
    '6:51': "\"The bread that I will give is my flesh.\" The sign of the loaves was always heading for the cross.",
    '6:52': "\"How can this man give us his flesh to eat?\" They take offence at the one point he will not soften.",
    '6:53': "Instead of explaining the hard saying he presses it harder, and adds the blood to the flesh.",
    '6:54': "Eating and drinking him is put in the present tense, with the resurrection promised at the far end.",
    '6:55': "\"Meat indeed... drink indeed.\" Whatever else this is, it is not a figure emptied of substance.",
    '6:56': "He dwells in us and we in him. The feeding ends in indwelling, not merely in benefit received.",
    '6:57': "As he lives by the Father, so the one who feeds on him lives by him. Life is always derived life.",
    '6:58': "The contrast closes where it began: manna and graves on one side, this bread and for ever on the other.",
    '6:60': "\"This is an hard saying.\" They do not complain that it is obscure. They understood and disliked it.",
    '6:61': "\"Doth this offend you?\" He asks whether they are offended rather than assuring them they need not be.",
    '6:62': "The ascension is put forward as the harder saying still. He does not mark the price down.",
    '6:63': "\"The words that I speak unto you, they are spirit, and they are life.\" The word carries what it says.",
    '6:64': "He knew from the beginning which of them did not believe, and went on teaching them anyway.",
    '6:65': "He repeats the hardest line at the precise moment the crowd is walking out over it.",
    '6:66': "\"Many of his disciples went back, and walked no more with him.\" A sermon that emptied the room.",
    '6:67': "\"Will ye also go away?\" He holds the door open for the twelve rather than holding on to them.",
    '6:68': "\"To whom shall we go?\" Peter stays not because it got easier but because there is nowhere else.",
    '6:69': "A confession made after the crowd has thinned is worth more than one made inside it.",
    '6:70': "He chose the twelve knowing what one of them was. The choosing was not an oversight.",
    '6:71': "John names Judas here, long before the night, so no reader can imagine Jesus was caught out.",

    # ---- Chapter 7 ----
    '7:1': "He keeps out of Judaea because they mean to kill him. Prudence is not the same thing as fear.",
    '7:3': "His brothers recommend a publicity campaign. The advice is worldly and comes from inside the family.",
    '7:4': "\"Shew thyself to the world.\" Sound counsel for building a following, and no use at all to him.",
    '7:5': "\"Neither did his brethren believe in him.\" Growing up in the same house converted nobody.",
    '7:6': "\"Your time is alway ready.\" Men with nothing at stake can move whenever they please.",
    '7:7': "The world cannot hate them; it hates him. Naming evil as evil is what earns the hatred.",
    '7:8': "He declines to be scheduled by his brothers. The hour is the Father's to set.",
    '7:10': "He goes up after all, but quietly and on his own timing rather than on theirs.",
    '7:12': "Good man or deceiver: the crowd splits immediately, and no third opinion is on offer.",
    '7:13': "\"No man spake openly of him for fear.\" The murmuring is loud and the confession silent.",
    '7:15': "\"Having never learned.\" They cannot place his credentials, so they need not weigh his words.",
    '7:16': "\"My doctrine is not mine, but his that sent me.\" He claims no originality, and that is the claim.",
    '7:17': "Willingness to do God's will comes before knowing the teaching is God's. Obedience precedes certainty.",
    '7:18': "Whose glory a man seeks is the test of whether he is telling the truth.",
    '7:19': "They defend a law none of them keeps, and are planning a murder while they do it.",
    '7:20': "\"Thou hast a devil.\" He is accused of imagining a plot that the reader has already been shown.",
    '7:21': "One work, and the city is still arguing about it months afterwards.",
    '7:22': "He meets them on their own ground: they themselves circumcise on the sabbath without a qualm.",
    '7:23': "If cutting a man is allowed to keep the law, making a man whole can hardly be breaking it.",
    '7:24': "\"Judge not according to the appearance.\" The prohibition is not on judging but on judging shallowly.",
    '7:25': "The locals know about the plot. The secret was never much of a secret.",
    '7:26': "\"Do the rulers know indeed that this is the very Christ?\" Official silence is read as quiet approval.",
    '7:27': "They disqualify him by a folk theory about the Messiah's hidden origins. Bad theology, held firmly.",
    '7:28': "\"He that sent me is true, whom ye know not.\" They know his town and not the one who sent him.",
    '7:29': "\"I know him: for I am from him.\" His knowledge of the Father is not study but origin.",
    '7:30': "They reached for him and could not lay a hand on him. Nothing touches Jesus before his hour.",
    '7:31': "Faith that arrives by counting miracles is still faith, and John records it without rebuke.",
    '7:32': "Officers are sent the moment the crowd begins to sway. It was always about control.",
    '7:33': "\"Yet a little while am I with you.\" He counts the remaining days in front of the men sent to seize him.",
    '7:34': "\"Where I am, thither ye cannot come.\" A seeking that comes too late is treated as a real possibility.",
    '7:35': "They guess he means the Gentile dispersion, and unknowingly describe what the gospel will do.",
    '7:37': "On the day water was poured at the altar, he stands up and offers himself as the drink.",
    '7:38': "The believer becomes a channel rather than a cistern. Rivers flow out of him, not into him.",
    '7:39': "John stops to explain: the Spirit waited on the glorifying, and in this Gospel that means the cross.",
    '7:41': "\"Shall Christ come out of Galilee?\" The objection is geography, and it settles the matter for them.",
    '7:42': "They quote Bethlehem against him without ever troubling to ask where he was born.",
    '7:43': "\"There was a division among the people because of him.\" He is the dividing line, not a topic beside it.",
    '7:45': "The officers come back with nobody, and now have to account for themselves.",
    '7:46': "\"Never man spake like this man.\" The arresting party is disarmed by the sound of him.",
    '7:47': "\"Are ye also deceived?\" An unwelcome report is answered with a question about the reporter.",
    '7:48': "\"Have any of the rulers believed on him?\" Truth settled by a show of hands among the important.",
    '7:49': "\"This people who knoweth not the law are cursed.\" Contempt for the ignorant, dressed as orthodoxy.",
    '7:51': "Nicodemus asks only that the law's own procedure be followed, and even that is too much.",
    '7:52': "\"Search, and look.\" They tell him to check the scriptures, having plainly not checked them.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 8 ----
    '8:2': "He comes back at daybreak and sits down to teach. The trap will be carried in mid-lesson.",
    '8:3': "\"They set her in the midst.\" A woman is made an exhibit in an argument about somebody else.",
    '8:4': "\"Taken in adultery, in the very act.\" The detail is supplied with something close to relish.",
    '8:5': "\"But what sayest thou?\" The law of Moses is quoted in order to trap the one who gave it.",
    '8:6': "\"Tempting him, that they might have to accuse him.\" Her sin is the pretext; he is the target.",
    '8:7': "\"He that is without sin among you, let him first cast a stone.\" The law is not waived but turned round.",
    '8:8': "He stoops and writes a second time, leaving them alone with the sentence he has just spoken.",
    '8:9': "\"Beginning at the eldest.\" The men who know themselves best are the first to walk away.",
    '8:10': "\"Where are those thine accusers?\" Asked of a woman with nothing at all to say in her defence.",
    '8:11': "\"Neither do I condemn thee: go, and sin no more.\" The absolution comes first and the command after.",
    '8:12': "\"I am the light of the world.\" Not a lamp for the devout but light the whole world may walk by.",
    '8:13': "They attack the form of his testimony because they have no answer to its content.",
    '8:14': "His witness holds because he knows where he came from and where he goes. They know neither.",
    '8:15': "\"Ye judge after the flesh.\" Judgment by appearance is the one kind he did not come to make.",
    '8:16': "Even his judging is not solitary. He and the Father are not two opinions but one.",
    '8:17': "He answers their legal objection out of their own statute about two witnesses.",
    '8:18': "The two witnesses are himself and the Father. That is either blasphemy or it is true.",
    '8:19': "\"Where is thy Father?\" They want an address. Knowing the Son is the only road to the Father.",
    '8:20': "He teaches in the treasury, well within reach, and nobody touches him. The hour governs everything.",
    '8:21': "\"Ye shall die in your sins.\" Said plainly, not as a threat but as a description of where they are going.",
    '8:22': "\"Will he kill himself?\" They reach for the ugliest available reading and think it shrewd.",
    '8:23': "From beneath and from above. What separates them is not moral distance but origin.",
    '8:24': "\"If ye believe not that I am he.\" What kills is not the catalogue of sins but the refusal of him.",
    '8:25': "\"Who art thou?\" Asked after chapters of answer. He has not altered his account since the beginning.",
    '8:26': "He speaks to the world only what he has heard. The messenger adds nothing of his own to it.",
    '8:27': "\"They understood not that he spake to them of the Father.\" The whole conversation goes past them.",
    '8:28': "\"When ye have lifted up the Son of man, then shall ye know.\" The cross is where the identity becomes legible.",
    '8:29': "\"The Father hath not left me alone.\" Said by the one who will yet cry that he has been forsaken.",
    '8:30': "Many believed while he was speaking. The rest of the chapter tests what that believing was worth.",
    '8:31': "\"If ye continue in my word.\" Continuing is the mark of a disciple, not the price of becoming one.",
    '8:32': "Truth known, and then freedom. Freedom here is a consequence, never the starting condition.",
    '8:33': "\"We were never in bondage to any man.\" Said under Roman rule. Slavery is hardest to see from inside.",
    '8:34': "\"Whosoever committeth sin is the servant of sin.\" The bondage is not imposed; it is chosen daily.",
    '8:35': "A slave has no permanent place in the house. Standing that rests on usefulness is never secure.",
    '8:36': "\"Free indeed.\" The freeing is the Son's work, done for people who did not think they needed it.",
    '8:37': "Abraham's seed by blood and murderous in intent, because the word found nowhere in them to lodge.",
    '8:38': "Two fathers, two sets of works. Paternity is shown here by what a man does, not by his descent.",
    '8:39': "\"If ye were Abraham's children, ye would do the works of Abraham.\" A claimed lineage is tested by conduct.",
    '8:40': "\"This did not Abraham.\" The father they claim never tried to kill a man for telling the truth.",
    '8:41': "\"We be not born of fornication.\" Even the insult they throw back is aimed at his birth.",
    '8:42': "\"If God were your Father, ye would love me.\" Love of Christ is the test of any claim to know God.",
    '8:43': "They cannot hear his word, so his speech makes no sense to them. The fault is deeper than comprehension.",
    '8:44': "A murderer and a liar from the beginning. The hardest sentence in the Gospel, said to religious men.",
    '8:45': "\"Because I tell you the truth, ye believe me not.\" The truth itself is what they are refusing.",
    '8:46': "\"Which of you convinceth me of sin?\" The challenge is thrown open and nobody takes it up.",
    '8:47': "Hearing God's words is the evidence of being God's. The inability is the diagnosis.",
    '8:48': "Out of arguments, they fall back on a racial slur and a charge of madness.",
    '8:49': "\"I have not a devil; but I honour my Father.\" He answers the charge and lets the insult lie.",
    '8:50': "\"I seek not mine own glory: there is one that seeketh.\" He leaves his vindication in other hands.",
    '8:51': "\"He shall never see death.\" Death becomes a thing the believer passes through without meeting.",
    '8:52': "Abraham died, therefore the claim is madness. The reasoning is sound and the premise is wrong.",
    '8:53': "\"Whom makest thou thyself?\" Exactly the right question, asked in entirely the wrong spirit.",
    '8:54': "Honour a man gives himself is worth nothing. He rests on a Father they claim and do not know.",
    '8:55': "He will not deny knowing the Father to keep the peace. Lying about God is not available to him.",
    '8:56': "\"Abraham rejoiced to see my day.\" The promise Abraham believed turns out to have a face.",
    '8:57': "\"Thou art not yet fifty years old.\" They work out his age and miss entirely what he said.",
    '8:58': "\"Before Abraham was, I am.\" Not \"I was\" — the tense is the divine name, and they heard it as such.",
    '8:59': "They took up stones, which is the clearest evidence that they understood him correctly.",

    # ---- Chapter 9 ----
    '9:1': "\"He saw a man which was blind from his birth.\" The seeing is Jesus' side of it; the man asked nothing.",
    '9:2': "The disciples see a case to be explained. Suffering is treated as a puzzle with a culprit in it.",
    '9:3': "Neither the man nor his parents. He refuses the ledger and points instead at what God will do.",
    '9:4': "\"The night cometh, when no man can work.\" Even his own labour is set inside a bounded day.",
    '9:5': "\"As long as I am in the world, I am the light of the world.\" Said immediately before he gives sight.",
    '9:6': "Spittle and clay. The one who made man out of dust reaches for dust again.",
    '9:7': "He is sent to a pool called Sent, and obeys an instruction he could not possibly have understood.",
    '9:8': "The neighbours cannot agree that it is the same man. A healed beggar is no longer recognizable.",
    '9:9': "\"I am he.\" He has to insist on his own identity to people who have watched him for years.",
    '9:11': "\"A man that is called Jesus.\" His entire theology at this point is a name and what happened to him.",
    '9:12': "\"Where is he? I know not.\" He has been healed by someone he has still never laid eyes on.",
    '9:14': "\"It was the sabbath day.\" John drops in the detail that turns a mercy into a court case.",
    '9:15': "Asked again, he shortens the story rather than decorating it. The facts need no help from him.",
    '9:16': "One party judges by the rule kept, the other by the work done, and the council splits in half.",
    '9:17': "Pressed to say what he thinks, the man goes further than before: \"He is a prophet.\"",
    '9:18': "They decline to believe he was ever blind. Denying the miracle is easier than facing it.",
    '9:19': "The parents are interrogated as though the healing were a family conspiracy.",
    '9:20': "They confirm what cannot be denied and volunteer not one syllable past it.",
    '9:21': "\"He is of age; ask him.\" Fear produces careful, truthful, entirely useless answers.",
    '9:22': "Excommunication had been agreed in advance. The verdict was settled before any evidence was heard.",
    '9:24': "\"Give God the praise: we know that this man is a sinner.\" Piety recruited to lean on a witness.",
    '9:25': "\"Whereas I was blind, now I see.\" Testimony that claims nothing beyond what it actually has.",
    '9:26': "They put the same question again, hoping the story will shift under repetition.",
    '9:27': "\"Will ye also be his disciples?\" The beggar has begun to mock the theologians.",
    '9:28': "\"We are Moses' disciples.\" They set Moses against Christ, which is the one thing Moses never did.",
    '9:29': "\"We know not from whence he is.\" Their own ignorance is produced as though it settled the matter.",
    '9:30': "\"Herein is a marvellous thing.\" The uneducated man puts his finger on the hole in their case.",
    '9:31': "He reasons from the theology he has: God does not hear sinners, and this man was plainly heard.",
    '9:32': "Since the world began nobody has opened eyes born blind. The sheer scale of it is his argument.",
    '9:33': "\"If this man were not of God, he could do nothing.\" A plain inference they cannot answer.",
    '9:34': "\"Thou wast altogether born in sins.\" They return to the theory Jesus refused, and throw him out.",
    '9:35': "Jesus goes looking for him the moment he is put out. The finding follows the cost of confessing.",
    '9:36': "\"Who is he, Lord, that I might believe on him?\" Willingness running ahead of knowledge.",
    '9:37': "\"It is he that talketh with thee.\" He finally sees the face of the one who gave him eyes.",
    '9:38': "\"Lord, I believe. And he worshipped him.\" Worship offered to a man, and not refused.",
    '9:39': "The seeing made blind and the blind made to see. Judgment is the sorting the light performs.",
    '9:40': "\"Are we blind also?\" Asked in the confidence that the answer could only be no.",
    '9:41': "Claiming to see is what holds the sin in place. Admitted blindness would have been curable.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 10 ----
    '10:1': "Climbing in some other way marks a man out. How he came in tells you what he is.",
    '10:3': "\"He calleth his own sheep by name.\" The shepherd's knowledge is particular, not a headcount.",
    '10:4': "He goes before them. The shepherd is not driving the flock along from behind.",
    '10:5': "They flee a stranger. Safety lies in knowing one voice well, not in assessing all the others.",
    '10:6': "\"They understood not.\" John records the failure so no reader assumes this was obvious.",
    '10:7': "\"I am the door of the sheep.\" Not merely the guide to the fold — he is the way in.",
    '10:8': "Those who came before him came to take. That the sheep did not hear them was their protection.",
    '10:9': "In and out, and pasture found. Salvation is described as somewhere to live, not only an escape.",
    '10:10': "The thief's whole business is subtraction. His is life, and more of it than was there before.",
    '10:11': "\"The good shepherd giveth his life for the sheep.\" The goodness is defined by the dying, not by skill.",
    '10:12': "The hireling runs because the sheep are not his. Ownership is what holds a man in place.",
    '10:13': "\"He careth not for the sheep.\" Paid work and love are not the same, and the wolf finds out which is which.",
    '10:14': "Knowing and being known, both directions. This is acquaintance, not supervision.",
    '10:15': "The knowing between shepherd and sheep is measured by the knowing between Father and Son.",
    '10:16': "\"Other sheep I have, which are not of this fold.\" They are his already, before they have heard a word.",
    '10:17': "Laid down in order to be taken again. The dying was never the end of the arrangement.",
    '10:18': "\"No man taketh it from me.\" The cross is not done to him; it is something he does.",
    '10:20': "\"He hath a devil, and is mad.\" The quickest way to stop listening is to declare the speaker unwell.",
    '10:21': "Someone points back at the blind man's eyes. That healing keeps forcing its way into the argument.",
    '10:22': "The feast of dedication, and it was winter. A cleansed temple kept in memory while its true temple walks the porch.",
    '10:24': "\"Tell us plainly.\" Clarity demanded of a man who has been nothing but plain with them.",
    '10:25': "\"I told you, and ye believed not.\" The trouble was never that the answer had been withheld.",
    '10:26': "\"Ye believe not, because ye are not of my sheep.\" The order runs opposite to the one we assume.",
    '10:27': "Hear, know, follow. His sheep are described by what happens to them, not by what they manage.",
    '10:28': "\"They shall never perish.\" The keeping is in his hand, which is the only reason it can be promised.",
    '10:29': "Two hands, the Son's and the Father's, and nothing in creation strong enough to open either.",
    '10:30': "\"I and my Father are one.\" Five words, and the stones come up off the ground.",
    '10:31': "The plainness they asked for is answered with an attempted execution.",
    '10:32': "\"For which of those works do ye stone me?\" He makes them say aloud what the charge actually is.",
    '10:33': "\"Being a man, makest thyself God.\" They state the doctrine of the incarnation as an accusation.",
    '10:34': "He answers out of a psalm, arguing inside their own text rather than over the top of it.",
    '10:35': "\"The scripture cannot be broken.\" A whole argument is hung on one word in a psalm.",
    '10:36': "Sanctified and sent. The language is of commission, and Son of God is not a title he awarded himself.",
    '10:37': "\"If I do not the works of my Father, believe me not.\" He offers them a test he could fail.",
    '10:38': "Believe the works if you will not believe the man. He will take faith in at the tradesman's door.",
    '10:40': "He goes back across Jordan to where John baptized. The ministry retreats to where it started.",
    '10:41': "\"John did no miracle.\" The greatest prophet performed no sign, and everything he said proved true.",
    '10:42': "Faith takes root in the backwater after the capital has tried twice to stone him.",

    # ---- Chapter 11 ----
    '11:3': "\"He whom thou lovest is sick.\" They ask for nothing. They name the love and leave it with him.",
    '11:4': "\"Not unto death\" — said of a sickness that kills the man. It is a verdict on the end, not the middle.",
    '11:5': "John states the love and then reports the delay. The waiting is inside the love, not against it.",
    '11:6': "He stayed two days more. The delay is deliberate and is never once explained to the sisters.",
    '11:7': "\"Let us go into Judaea again\" — back to the place where they had lately tried to stone him.",
    '11:8': "The disciples remember the stones. Their objection is entirely reasonable and entirely beside the point.",
    '11:9': "Twelve hours in the day. There is time allotted for the work, and danger does not shorten it.",
    '11:10': "Stumbling comes of walking without light, not of walking somewhere dangerous.",
    '11:11': "\"Our friend Lazarus sleepeth.\" Death renamed by someone speaking from the far side of it.",
    '11:12': "\"If he sleep, he shall do well.\" They take real comfort from a complete misunderstanding.",
    '11:14': "\"Lazarus is dead.\" He drops the figure of speech the moment it stops helping them.",
    '11:15': "\"I am glad... to the intent ye may believe.\" Gladness about an absence that cost a family four days.",
    '11:16': "Thomas proposes going along to die with him. Loyal, gloomy, and entirely in earnest.",
    '11:17': "Four days in the grave. John counts them so that nobody can call this a swoon.",
    '11:19': "The house is full of mourners. What is about to be undone is a normal, thorough funeral.",
    '11:20': "Martha goes out to meet him; Mary stays sitting. Grief does not make people behave alike.",
    '11:21': "\"Lord, if thou hadst been here.\" Complaint and faith in one sentence, said to his face.",
    '11:22': "\"Even now.\" She will not name what she hopes for, but she has not closed the door on it.",
    '11:23': "\"Thy brother shall rise again.\" Offered as comfort and meant as a plan for that afternoon.",
    '11:24': "She files it under the last day. Sound doctrine can be a way of expecting nothing at present.",
    '11:25': "\"I am the resurrection, and the life.\" Not a doctrine she must hold but a person standing in front of her.",
    '11:26': "\"Believest thou this?\" The question is put to a woman with a four-day-old grave in sight.",
    '11:27': "She confesses more than she understands, and the confession is taken exactly as it stands.",
    '11:28': "\"The Master is come, and calleth for thee.\" The summons is private, and a sister carries it.",
    '11:31': "The mourners follow, assuming she is going to weep. They end up witnesses instead.",
    '11:32': "Mary says precisely what Martha said, and then nothing further. She only weeps.",
    '11:33': "He groaned in the spirit and was troubled. The one about to undo death is angry at the grave.",
    '11:35': "\"Jesus wept.\" He weeps minutes before he removes the cause of the weeping.",
    '11:36': "\"Behold how he loved him.\" For once the onlookers read the tears correctly.",
    '11:37': "Others read the same tears as failure. Weeping is taken as evidence in both directions.",
    '11:38': "He comes to the cave groaning still. He walks into the place of death grieving, not composed.",
    '11:39': "\"By this time he stinketh.\" Martha, who has just confessed him, states what everyone is thinking.",
    '11:40': "\"If thou wouldest believe, thou shouldest see.\" Believing comes first; seeing follows it.",
    '11:41': "He thanks the Father before anything happens. The answer was settled before the words were said.",
    '11:42': "The prayer is spoken out loud for the crowd's benefit. He prays so that they will hear him praying.",
    '11:43': "\"Lazarus, come forth.\" A name called into a tomb, and that is the whole of what it took.",
    '11:44': "\"Loose him, and let him go.\" He raises the man and leaves the graveclothes to the bystanders.",
    '11:45': "Many believed. The sign did precisely the work it was given to do.",
    '11:46': "Others went and reported it. The same miracle produces a convert and an informer.",
    '11:47': "\"This man doeth many miracles.\" They never dispute the miracles, only what follows from them.",
    '11:48': "\"Our place and nation.\" The calculation is about what they stand to lose, and they lose it anyway.",
    '11:49': "\"Ye know nothing at all.\" Contempt is his opening argument, and it carries the room.",
    '11:50': "\"Expedient that one man should die for the people.\" Political arithmetic that states the atonement exactly.",
    '11:51': "John says the high priest prophesied. The truth comes out of the mouth arranging the murder.",
    '11:52': "The gathering is wider than Caiaphas meant: the scattered children of God, not one nation's safety.",
    '11:53': "\"From that day forth.\" A man raised from the dead is what finally decides them to kill him.",
    '11:55': "They go up early to purify themselves for the passover, with the warrant already drawn up.",
    '11:56': "The temple talk is whether he will risk coming to the feast. He will.",
    '11:57': "A standing order to inform on him. The machinery is in place before the week has even begun.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 12 ----
    '12:1': "Six days before the passover. From here John is counting down to the hour.",
    '12:2': "Martha serving, Lazarus at the table. The raised man's life is ordinary again, and that is the point.",
    '12:3': "A year's wages poured out in a minute, and the smell of it fills the whole house.",
    '12:5': "\"Sold for three hundred pence, and given to the poor.\" The most respectable objection in the room.",
    '12:6': "\"Not that he cared for the poor; but because he was a thief.\" Concern for the needy makes good cover.",
    '12:7': "\"Against the day of my burying hath she kept this.\" She has read the week better than the twelve have.",
    '12:8': "\"Me ye have not always.\" The poor will still be here tomorrow; he will not.",
    '12:9': "They come partly to look at Lazarus. Even a resurrection becomes a sight worth visiting.",
    '12:10': "They plan to kill Lazarus too. The evidence must be destroyed along with the man who produced it.",
    '12:11': "A living man was making converts simply by being alive in front of people.",
    '12:13': "Palms, Hosanna, King of Israel. They are shouting for the right man on entirely the wrong terms.",
    '12:14': "He finds a young ass. The royal procession is deliberately too small for the noise around it.",
    '12:15': "\"Fear not, daughter of Sion.\" The King arrives in a manner calculated to frighten nobody.",
    '12:16': "They understood it only afterwards, and then found they had been acting inside a prophecy.",
    '12:17': "The witnesses of Lazarus keep telling it. The crowd is largely built out of their testimony.",
    '12:19': "\"The world is gone after him.\" Despair from the Pharisees, and a prophecy they did not intend.",
    '12:20': "Greeks at the feast. The world starts arriving before the hour has even struck.",
    '12:21': "\"Sir, we would see Jesus.\" The simplest request in the Gospel, and it comes through a middleman.",
    '12:23': "Gentiles ask for him and he answers that the hour has come. Their asking is the signal.",
    '12:24': "A grain that will not die stays one grain. Fruitfulness runs through burial, never around it.",
    '12:25': "Loving your life is precisely how it is lost. The arithmetic of self-preservation does not work.",
    '12:26': "\"Where I am, there shall also my servant be.\" What the servant is promised is a place, not a wage.",
    '12:27': "\"Now is my soul troubled.\" John has no garden agony and sets it here instead, out in the open.",
    '12:28': "\"Father, glorify thy name.\" The prayer he settles on asks for God's glory, not for his own rescue.",
    '12:29': "Thunder, or an angel. A voice from heaven is heard and explained away in the same moment.",
    '12:30': "\"This voice came not because of me, but for your sakes.\" He did not need the confirmation himself.",
    '12:31': "The judgment of the world and the casting out of its prince are both dated to the cross.",
    '12:32': "\"Lifted up\" means crucified, and it is the lifting up itself that does the drawing.",
    '12:33': "John refuses to let the metaphor float free. He states what kind of death was meant.",
    '12:34': "\"Who is this Son of man?\" Their scripture says Christ abides; a dying Messiah will not fit the slot.",
    '12:35': "\"Walk while ye have the light.\" The light is not permanently on offer and the window is short.",
    '12:36': "He tells them to believe in the light, and then hides himself. The offer is real and it is not endless.",
    '12:37': "So many miracles, and still they did not believe. John states it as the plain result of the ministry.",
    '12:38': "Isaiah asked who had believed the report. The answer, then and since, is disappointingly few.",
    '12:40': "Blinded eyes and hardened hearts. John will not soften the hardest thing scripture says about unbelief.",
    '12:41': "\"Esaias... saw his glory, and spake of him.\" The glory filling the temple in that vision was Christ's.",
    '12:42': "Many of the rulers believed and said nothing. Silent faith kept their seats in the synagogue.",
    '12:43': "\"They loved the praise of men more than the praise of God.\" The whole of cowardice in one line.",
    '12:44': "Believing on him is believing on the one who sent him. He will not be received in isolation.",
    '12:45': "\"He that seeth me seeth him that sent me.\" There is no looking past Christ to a God standing behind him.",
    '12:46': "He came as light so that believers need not remain in the dark. The remaining is what he came to end.",
    '12:47': "\"I came not to judge the world, but to save the world.\" The first coming has one stated purpose.",
    '12:48': "The word already spoken will do the judging. Rejection is not the absence of a verdict but a delayed one.",
    '12:49': "He speaks under commandment. Nothing in the message is his own invention or addition.",
    '12:50': "\"His commandment is life everlasting.\" Even the commanding is aimed at handing out life.",

    # ---- Chapter 13 ----
    '13:1': "\"He loved them unto the end.\" Written over a chapter containing the betrayer and the denier.",
    '13:2': "The devil has already put it into Judas' heart, and the supper goes on as though nothing were wrong.",
    '13:3': "Knowing all things were in his hands, he reaches for a towel. Security is what makes stooping possible.",
    '13:4': "He lays aside his garments. The gesture is the whole incarnation performed in a room.",
    '13:5': "He washes their feet, Judas' among them. The service is not sorted by who deserves it.",
    '13:6': "\"Lord, dost thou wash my feet?\" Peter's objection sounds like reverence and functions as refusal.",
    '13:7': "\"Thou shalt know hereafter.\" He does not explain first; understanding is allowed to arrive late.",
    '13:8': "\"If I wash thee not, thou hast no part with me.\" To refuse his service is to refuse him.",
    '13:9': "Peter overcorrects and asks for the whole bath. Anything rather than simply being served.",
    '13:10': "\"Ye are clean, but not all.\" Daily washing of the feet is not the washing that made them clean.",
    '13:11': "He knew who it was, and washed that man's feet along with the rest.",
    '13:12': "\"Know ye what I have done to you?\" The lesson is not left to be inferred from the atmosphere.",
    '13:13': "\"Ye say well; for so I am.\" He does not disclaim the title of Lord while kneeling on the floor.",
    '13:14': "The washing is given as a duty, not a mood. Lordship is the reason to stoop, not an exemption.",
    '13:15': "\"I have given you an example.\" Not a ceremony to reproduce but a shape of life to copy.",
    '13:16': "No servant outranks his lord, which settles what work can be considered beneath anyone.",
    '13:17': "Knowing is not where the blessing is. Doing is, and he puts the gap between them in plain words.",
    '13:18': "\"He that eateth bread with me hath lifted up his heel against me.\" The betrayal happens at table.",
    '13:19': "He tells them beforehand so the worst night of their lives will not look like an accident.",
    '13:20': "Receiving the sent one is receiving him, and receiving him is receiving the Father. The chain holds.",
    '13:21': "\"He was troubled in spirit.\" Knowing it was coming did not make the betrayal hurt any less.",
    '13:22': "They look round at one another, doubting. Nobody in the room can pick Judas out.",
    '13:23': "The disciple whom Jesus loved is identified by that alone, and never by his own name.",
    '13:24': "Peter beckons instead of asking. Even he has gone quiet by this point in the evening.",
    '13:25': "\"Lord, who is it?\" The question is asked from close enough to lean against him.",
    '13:26': "The dipped sop was a host's mark of honour, and it is handed straight to the traitor.",
    '13:27': "\"That thou doest, do quickly.\" The betrayal proceeds on Christ's timetable, not Satan's.",
    '13:28': "Nobody at the table understood. The most decisive exit in history looked like an errand.",
    '13:29': "They assume he has gone to buy provisions or give to the poor. Evil seldom announces itself.",
    '13:30': "\"He went immediately out: and it was night.\" John means a good deal more than the hour.",
    '13:31': "With Judas gone he calls it glory. The betrayal is counted as the start of the glorifying.",
    '13:32': "\"Straightway.\" The glorifying is not held back until after the cross; it runs through it.",
    '13:33': "\"Little children.\" The tenderness comes exactly as he tells them they cannot follow him.",
    '13:34': "What is new is the measure: as I have loved you. The command is old; the standard is not.",
    '13:35': "The mark of discipleship is not doctrine held or zeal shown but love among themselves.",
    '13:36': "\"Thou shalt follow me afterwards.\" Peter's martyrdom is promised in the same breath as his delay.",
    '13:37': "\"I will lay down my life for thy sake.\" He means it, and his life is not the one that is needed.",
    '13:38': "The denial is foretold to his face, and he stays at the table afterwards all the same.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 14 ----
    '14:1': "\"Let not your heart be troubled.\" Said to men who have just heard of the betrayal and the denial.",
    '14:2': "\"If it were not so, I would have told you.\" The promise rests on his refusal ever to flatter them.",
    '14:3': "\"That where I am, there ye may be also.\" Heaven is described as nearness to him, nothing else.",
    '14:4': "\"The way ye know.\" He credits them with more than they have, and the question comes out.",
    '14:5': "Thomas admits he is lost. That admission earns the clearest answer in the book.",
    '14:6': "\"I am the way, the truth, and the life.\" Not a route described but a person to be travelled by.",
    '14:7': "Knowing the Son is knowing the Father. There is no further acquaintance to be made elsewhere.",
    '14:8': "\"Shew us the Father, and it sufficeth us.\" Philip asks for one thing more while standing beside it.",
    '14:9': "\"Have I been so long time with you?\" The gentlest rebuke in the Gospel, and one of the saddest.",
    '14:10': "The Father dwelling in him does the works. Even here the Son claims nothing as his own doing.",
    '14:11': "\"Or else believe me for the very works' sake.\" He lets faith come in by the lower door.",
    '14:12': "\"Greater works\" — because he goes to the Father, not because the disciples improve on him.",
    '14:13': "Asking in his name means asking as he would ask, and the stated aim is the Father's glory.",
    '14:14': "The promise is repeated bare, in a room full of men who are hours from failing completely.",
    '14:15': "Love first and obedience after. The order is never once reversed in this Gospel.",
    '14:16': "\"Another Comforter\" — of the same kind as himself, and given to stay rather than to visit.",
    '14:17': "The world cannot receive him because it cannot see him. The Spirit is known by indwelling, not inspection.",
    '14:18': "\"I will not leave you comfortless.\" The word is orphans. The household is not being broken up.",
    '14:19': "\"Because I live, ye shall live also.\" Their life hangs on his, not on their hold of it.",
    '14:20': "In the Father, in him, in us. The indwelling is a chain, and we are on the end of it.",
    '14:21': "Keeping his words is the evidence of love; being loved and shown himself is what follows.",
    '14:22': "\"Why unto us, and not unto the world?\" The question every hidden generation has wanted to ask.",
    '14:23': "\"We will come unto him, and make our abode with him.\" God answers hiddenness by moving in.",
    '14:24': "The words are the Father's. Setting them aside is not a difference of opinion with Jesus.",
    '14:26': "The Comforter teaches and reminds. The apostolic memory is not left to unaided recall.",
    '14:27': "\"Not as the world giveth.\" The world's peace is the absence of trouble; his is given inside it.",
    '14:28': "\"If ye loved me, ye would rejoice.\" Their grief is real and still partly about themselves.",
    '14:29': "Told in advance so that the fulfilment produces faith instead of bewilderment.",
    '14:30': "\"The prince of this world cometh, and hath nothing in me.\" He is searched and nothing is found.",
    '14:31': "\"Arise, let us go hence.\" He walks towards the arrest so the world may know he loves the Father.",

    # ---- Chapter 15 ----
    '15:1': "\"The true vine.\" Israel was the vine that failed; he takes the name and makes it good.",
    '15:2': "Fruitful branches get cut as well. The knife is a sign of intention, not of displeasure.",
    '15:3': "\"Ye are clean through the word which I have spoken.\" The pruning is done by what he says.",
    '15:4': "\"Abide in me, and I in you.\" The command is to stay where they were put, not to produce.",
    '15:5': "\"Without me ye can do nothing.\" Not less and not worse — nothing. A branch has no supply of its own.",
    '15:6': "A branch that does not abide withers by itself. Nobody has to make it useless.",
    '15:7': "Prayer that grows out of abiding asks for the kind of thing that gets granted.",
    '15:8': "The Father is glorified by fruit borne on branches. The credit runs back down to the root.",
    '15:9': "His love for them is measured by the Father's love for him. The scale is set impossibly high.",
    '15:10': "Abiding in love is not passive warmth; he puts his own obedience forward as the pattern of it.",
    '15:11': "\"That my joy might remain in you.\" The whole farewell is spoken with their joy as its object.",
    '15:12': "\"As I have loved you.\" The measure is given the night before he demonstrates what it cost.",
    '15:13': "\"Greater love hath no man than this.\" Said hours before doing it, by one for whom it was not the limit.",
    '15:14': "\"Ye are my friends, if ye do whatsoever I command you.\" Friendship here does not dissolve the command.",
    '15:15': "Servants are kept in the dark; friends are told. He has withheld nothing he heard from the Father.",
    '15:16': "\"Ye have not chosen me, but I have chosen you.\" The initiative is settled, and it was never ours.",
    '15:18': "\"It hated me before it hated you.\" The hatred is inherited, not earned by anything they have done.",
    '15:19': "Being chosen out of the world is what makes the world hostile. Belonging elsewhere is the offence.",
    '15:20': "The servant is not greater than his lord — said again, now about persecution rather than washing feet.",
    '15:21': "All of it is done for his name's sake, by people who do not know the one who sent him.",
    '15:22': "\"They have no cloak for their sin.\" The coming of Christ takes away the excuse of not knowing.",
    '15:23': "Hatred of the Son is hatred of the Father. There is no reverent way to reject Jesus.",
    '15:24': "They saw the works and hated him anyway. Evidence was never the thing that was missing.",
    '15:25': "\"They hated me without a cause.\" Even motiveless hatred had already been written down.",
    '15:26': "The Spirit's work is to testify of Christ. He does not come to draw attention to himself.",
    '15:27': "\"Ye also shall bear witness.\" The Spirit's testimony arrives through men who were actually there.",

    # ---- Chapter 16 ----
    '16:1': "\"That ye should not be offended.\" The warning is given ahead of time as a kindness.",
    '16:2': "Men will kill them believing they do God service. Sincerity is no protection against atrocity.",
    '16:3': "The persecutors act out of not knowing the Father. Ignorance explains it without excusing it.",
    '16:4': "He held these things back while he was with them. There is a right time for hard information.",
    '16:5': "\"None of you asketh me, Whither goest thou?\" Their sorrow has made them incurious about him.",
    '16:6': "\"Sorrow hath filled your heart.\" He names their grief instead of arguing them out of it.",
    '16:7': "\"It is expedient for you that I go away.\" His absence will serve them better than his presence.",
    '16:8': "The Spirit's first work in the world is conviction: sin, righteousness, judgment, in that order.",
    '16:9': "The sin in question is unbelief. Everything else in the catalogue runs downstream of it.",
    '16:10': "Righteousness is proved by his going to the Father. The vindication is an empty place, not a verdict.",
    '16:11': "Judgment is settled because the prince of this world has already been judged and is going out.",
    '16:12': "\"Ye cannot bear them now.\" He measures what he says by what they are able to carry.",
    '16:13': "The Spirit speaks nothing of himself. Even within God the pattern is saying what has been heard.",
    '16:14': "\"He shall glorify me.\" A work of the Spirit that does not point at Christ is not his work.",
    '16:15': "What the Spirit shows is Christ's, and what is Christ's is the Father's. Nothing new is introduced.",
    '16:16': "\"A little while,\" twice: one interval covers the grave and the other covers the whole age.",
    '16:17': "They confer among themselves instead of asking him. Confusion prefers company to questions.",
    '16:18': "\"We cannot tell what he saith.\" John lets the disciples look exactly as lost as they were.",
    '16:19': "He knew they wanted to ask, and asked it for them. The question is put into their mouths.",
    '16:20': "Sorrow turned into joy, not swapped for it. The very thing that grieved them becomes the gladness.",
    '16:21': "The figure is childbirth: the pain is not cancelled but forgotten in what it produced.",
    '16:22': "\"Your joy no man taketh from you.\" Nobody can confiscate what nobody gave.",
    '16:23': "In that day the asking goes straight to the Father, in the Son's name.",
    '16:24': "\"Hitherto have ye asked nothing in my name.\" A whole manner of praying has not yet begun.",
    '16:25': "Plain speech is promised. What is obscure to them now is not permanently obscure.",
    '16:26': "He will not have to talk the Father round. The Son's asking is no shield from a reluctant God.",
    '16:27': "\"The Father himself loveth you.\" The love did not have to be extracted from him by anyone.",
    '16:28': "Came forth, came into, leave, go to. The whole Gospel in one sentence of four movements.",
    '16:29': "\"Now speakest thou plainly.\" They announce that they have grasped it, minutes before scattering.",
    '16:30': "Their confidence rests on his knowing them, and it will not survive the next few hours.",
    '16:31': "\"Do ye now believe?\" Not a compliment, and not quite a denial of what they have said either.",
    '16:32': "\"Ye shall leave me alone: and yet I am not alone.\" He states the desertion and forgives it in advance.",
    '16:33': "\"I have overcome the world.\" Past tense, said before the cross, with tribulation promised alongside.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 17 ----
    '17:1': "\"Father, the hour is come.\" The hour he kept deferring is now the thing he asks for.",
    '17:2': "Power over all flesh, used to give life to a particular number. Authority in the service of giving.",
    '17:3': "Eternal life is defined as knowing God and the one he sent. It is acquaintance, not duration.",
    '17:4': "\"I have finished the work.\" Said before the cross, of a thing already as good as done.",
    '17:5': "He asks for the glory he had before the world was. A request to have back, not to be given.",
    '17:6': "\"Thine they were, and thou gavest them me.\" The disciples were the Father's gift to the Son.",
    '17:7': "What they have grasped is that everything about him comes from the Father. That much has landed.",
    '17:8': "He gave them the words he was given. The chain of receiving runs all the way down to them.",
    '17:9': "\"I pray not for the world, but for them.\" At this hour his praying narrows to the men in the room.",
    '17:10': "\"I am glorified in them\" — said of eleven men who will scatter before the night is over.",
    '17:11': "\"Keep through thine own name.\" The keeping is asked of the Father, not demanded of the disciples.",
    '17:12': "\"None of them is lost, but the son of perdition.\" The exception is named, and it is not a slip in the keeping.",
    '17:13': "He prays out loud, in their hearing, so that his own joy may be filled up in them.",
    '17:14': "The world's hatred follows from having his word and belonging where he belongs.",
    '17:15': "Not taken out of the world but kept from the evil. Withdrawal was never on the table.",
    '17:16': "Not of the world, even as he is not. Their strangeness is entirely derived from his.",
    '17:17': "\"Sanctify them through thy truth: thy word is truth.\" The setting apart is done by the word.",
    '17:18': "As the Father sent him, so he sends them. The mission is handed on in the same shape it came.",
    '17:19': "He sets himself apart for the altar, and John records that he did it for their sakes.",
    '17:20': "The prayer widens to all who will believe through their word. It reaches the person reading it.",
    '17:21': "The unity asked for is modelled on Father and Son, and its purpose is that the world believe.",
    '17:22': "The glory given him he has already handed over. It is given away before it is fully received.",
    '17:23': "\"Hast loved them, as thou hast loved me.\" The same love, on the same terms, extended to us.",
    '17:24': "\"I will that they also be with me where I am.\" The last thing he asks for is our company.",
    '17:25': "\"O righteous Father, the world hath not known thee.\" He says it as a grief, not as an indictment.",
    '17:26': "\"And will declare it.\" The declaring does not stop at the cross; it carries on after him.",

    # ---- Chapter 18 ----
    '18:1': "He crosses the Cedron into a garden. John's passion begins in a garden and ends in one.",
    '18:2': "\"Judas also knew the place.\" The betrayal runs on knowledge that friendship gave him.",
    '18:3': "Lanterns, torches and weapons, to arrest a man who had been teaching daily in the temple.",
    '18:4': "\"Knowing all things that should come upon him, went forth.\" He walks out to meet the arrest.",
    '18:5': "\"I am he\" — the same words he spoke on the water, said now to a cohort carrying swords.",
    '18:6': "They went backward and fell to the ground. He could have ended it there, and lets them stand up.",
    '18:7': "He asks a second time, giving them their chance to complete an arrest that has just collapsed.",
    '18:8': "\"Let these go their way.\" His first concern as he is taken is the safety of the others.",
    '18:9': "John reads the rescue of eleven frightened men as the fulfilment of losing none.",
    '18:10': "Peter draws a sword and takes off an ear. Courage aimed in precisely the wrong direction.",
    '18:11': "\"The cup which my Father hath given me.\" He calls the arrest a gift from God, not a trick of Judas.",
    '18:12': "They bind him. Nothing holds him but what he has agreed to.",
    '18:13': "He goes to Annas first: an unofficial hearing before the official one, which is how it is arranged.",
    '18:14': "John reminds us that the verdict was drafted weeks ago by the man about to hear the case.",
    '18:15': "Two disciples follow. Following at a distance is how the denial gets started.",
    '18:16': "Another man's connections get Peter inside. The door opened for him is the door he falls through.",
    '18:17': "\"I am not.\" Peter's answer is the exact negative of the words Jesus has just used of himself.",
    '18:18': "\"Peter stood with them, and warmed himself.\" A cold night, an enemy's fire, and easy company.",
    '18:19': "The high priest asks about his disciples and his doctrine — a hearing conducted without witnesses.",
    '18:20': "\"In secret have I said nothing.\" There is no hidden teaching to be got out of him.",
    '18:21': "\"Ask them which heard me.\" He insists on the procedure the court is busy skipping.",
    '18:22': "A blow for a lawful answer. The first violence of the trial is over a point of etiquette.",
    '18:23': "\"Bear witness of the evil.\" He asks for evidence, even from the man who has just struck him.",
    '18:25': "While Jesus refuses to say anything false, Peter at the fire denies him a second time.",
    '18:26': "A kinsman of Malchus recognizes him. The sword stroke has come back around to Peter.",
    '18:27': "\"And immediately the cock crew.\" John adds nothing; the sound is the whole sentence.",
    '18:28': "They will not enter a Gentile hall lest they be defiled, while arranging a judicial murder.",
    '18:29': "\"What accusation bring ye against this man?\" Pilate asks the obvious question and never gets an answer.",
    '18:30': "\"If he were not a malefactor...\" Indignation is offered in place of a charge.",
    '18:31': "They need Rome because they want a death their own law will not let them carry out.",
    '18:32': "Needing a Roman execution is what makes it a cross, exactly as he had said it would be.",
    '18:33': "\"Art thou the King of the Jews?\" The first thing Rome wants to know about is a throne.",
    '18:34': "He asks Pilate whether the question is his own. The judge is being examined as well.",
    '18:35': "\"Am I a Jew?\" Pilate's contempt for the whole business is entirely undisguised.",
    '18:36': "\"My servants would fight.\" The evidence that his kingdom is not of this world is that nobody is fighting.",
    '18:37': "Born for this: to bear witness to the truth. The kingship is exercised by testifying.",
    '18:38': "\"What is truth?\" Asked of the truth, by a man who does not wait for the answer.",
    '18:39': "Pilate reaches for a loophole instead of an acquittal, having just said there is no fault.",
    '18:40': "\"Not this man, but Barabbas.\" A guilty man walks free because the innocent one takes his place.",
})
